<launch>
    <arg name="map_path" default="$(find puzzlebot_navigation2)/maps/map_maze.yaml" />
    <arg name="nav2_params_file" default="$(find puzzlebot_navigation2)/config/nav2_params.yaml" />
    <arg name="rviz_config_path" default="$(find puzzlebot_navigation2)/rviz/nav2.rviz" />
    <arg name="use_sim_time" default="true" />
    
    <include file="$(find nav2_bringup)/launch/bringup_launch.py"> 
        <arg name="map" value="$(var map_path)" />
        <arg name="params_file" value="$(var nav2_params_file)" />
        <arg name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)" />
    </include>

    <node pkg="rviz2" exec="rviz2" name="rviz2" output="screen" args="-d $(var rviz_config_path)">
        <param name="use_sim_time" value="$(var use_sim_time)" />
    </node>

</launch>

// The bringup is used to launch the navigation stack, and it will read the parameters from the nav2_params.yaml file
    
    To know what to reference for the bringup we can check the nav2_bringup package and see that the bringup_launch.py 
    file is looking for a map argument, so we can pass it the map_path argument that we defined at the beginning of this 
    launch file, as well as use_sim_time argument to use the simulation time, and the params_file argument to pass 
    the nav2_params.yaml file that we defined at the beginning of this launch file
    
    If we didn't use the bringup file we would need to launch the different nodes that are part of the navigation stack, 
    such as the map_server, amcl, and the different planners and controllers, 
    but by using the bringup file we can launch all of these nodes with a single command, 
    and we can also pass the parameters to these nodes through the nav2_params.yaml file.